cite125†zconf.h.in |    |\nL204: cite126†zlib.3 | cite126†zlib.3 |    |\nL205: cite127†zlib.3.pdf | cite127†zlib.3.pdf |    |\nL206: cite128†zlib.h | cite128†zlib.h |    |\nL207: cite129†zlib.map | cite129†zlib.map |    |\nL208: cite130†zlib.pc.cmakein | cite130†zlib.pc.cmakein |    |\nL209: cite131†zlib.pc.in | cite131†zlib.pc.in |    |\nL210: cite132†zlibConfig.cmake.in | cite132†zlibConfig.cmake.in |    |\nL211: cite133†zutil.c | cite133†zutil.c |    |\nL212: cite134†zutil.h | cite134†zutil.h |    |\nL213: [Button: View all files]\nL214: ## Repository files navigation\nL215: \nL216:   *   * cite135†README L217:   * cite135†License L218: More items\nL219: \nL220:     ZLIB DATA COMPRESSION LIBRARY\nL221: \nL222:     zlib 1.3.2 is a general purpose data compression library.  All the code is\nL223:     thread safe (though see the FAQ for caveats).  The data format used by the zlib\nL224:     library is described by RFCs (Request for Comments) 1950 to 1952 at\nL225:     cite136†https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1950†datatracker.ietf.org (zlib format), rfc1951 (deflate\nL226:     format) and rfc1952 (gzip format).\nL227:     All functions of the compression library are documented in the file zlib.h\nL228:     (volunteer to write man pages welcome, contact zlib@gzip.org).  A usage example\nL229:     of the library is given in the file test/example.c which also tests that\nL230:     the library is working correctly.  Another example is given in the file\nL231:     test/minigzip.c.  The compression library itself is composed of all source\nL232:     files in the root directory.\nL233:     To compile all files and run the test program, follow the instructions given at\nL234:     the top of Makefile.in.  In short \"./configure; make test\", and if that goes\nL235:     well, \"make install\" should work for most flavors of Unix.  For Windows, use\nL236:     one of the special makefiles in win32/ or contrib/vstudio/ .  For VMS, use\nL237:     make_vms.com.\nL238: \nL239:     Questions about zlib should be sent to <zlib@gzip.org>, or to Gilles Vollant\nL240:     <info@winimage.com> for the Windows DLL version.  The zlib home page is\nL241:     cite137†https://zlib.net/†zlib.net .  Before reporting a problem, please check this site to\nL242:     verify that you have the latest version of zlib; otherwise get the latest\nL243:     version and check whether the problem still exists or not.\nL244: \nL245:     PLEASE read the zlib FAQ cite138†https://zlib.net/zlib_faq.html†zlib.net before asking for help.\nL246: \nL247:     Mark Nelson <markn@ieee.org> wrote an article about zlib for the Jan.  1997\nL248:     issue of Dr.  Dobb's Journal; a copy of the article is available at\nL249:     cite139†https://zlib.net/nelson/†zlib.net .\nL250: \nL251:     The changes made in version 1.3.2 are documented in the file ChangeLog.\nL252: \nL253:     Unsupported third party contributions are provided in directory contrib/ .\nL254: \nL255:     zlib is available in Java using the java.util.zip package. Follow the API\nL256:     Documentation link at: cite140†https://docs.oracle.com/search/?q=java.util.zip†docs.oracle.com .\nL257: \nL258:     A Perl interface to zlib and bzip2 written by Paul Marquess <pmqs@cpan.org>\nL259:     can be found at cite141†https://github.com/pmqs/IO-Compress .\nL260: \nL261:     A Python interface to zlib written by A.M. Kuchling <amk@amk.ca> is\nL262:     available in Python 1.5 and later versions, see\nL263:     cite142†https://docs.python.org/3/library/zlib.html†docs.python.org .\nL264: \nL265:     zlib is built into tcl: cite143†https://wiki.tcl-lang.org/page/zlib†wiki.tcl-lang.org .\nL266: \nL267:     An experimental package to read and write files in .zip format, written on top\nL268:     of zlib by Gilles Vollant <info@winimage.com>, is available in the\nL269:     contrib/minizip directory of zlib.\nL270: \n"
